**Consumer side (e.g., Order Service consuming User Service)**

```java
@ExtendWith(PactConsumerTestExt.class)
@PactTestFor(providerName = "UserService", port = "8080")
public class UserClientContractTest {

    @Pact(consumer = "OrderService", provider = "UserService")
    public V4Pact getUserPact(PactDslWithProvider builder) {
        return builder
            .given("user 42 exists")
            .uponReceiving("get user 42 request")
                .method("GET")
                .path("/users/42")
                .headers("Accept", "application/json")
            .willRespondWith()
                .status(200)
                .headers("Content-Type", "application/json")
                .body(new PactDslJsonBody()
                    .integerType("id", 42)
                    .stringType("name", "Alice")
                    .stringType("email", "alice@example.com"))
            .toPact(V4Pact.class);
    }

    @Test
    @PactTestFor(pactMethod = "getUserPact")
    void shouldGetUser(MockServer mockServer) {
        UserClient client = new UserClient(mockServer.getUrl());
        User user = client.getUser(42L);

        assertThat(user.getId()).isEqualTo(42L);
        assertThat(user.getName()).isEqualTo("Alice");
        assertThat(user.getEmail()).isEqualTo("alice@example.com");
    }
}
```

Running this test generates `build/pacts/OrderService-UserService.json`:

```json
{
  "consumer": {"name": "OrderService"},
  "provider": {"name": "UserService"},
  "interactions": [{
    "description": "get user 42 request",
    "providerStates": [{"name": "user 42 exists"}],
    "request": {"method": "GET", "path": "/users/42"},
    "response": {"status": 200, "body": {"id": 42, "name": "Alice", "email": "alice@example.com"}}
  }]
}
```

Publish to Pact Broker:

```bash
pact-broker publish build/pacts \
  --consumer-app-version=$GIT_SHA \
  --branch=$GIT_BRANCH \
  --broker-base-url=https://pact-broker.internal
```

**Producer side (UserService)**

```java
@Provider("UserService")
@PactBroker(host = "pact-broker.internal", port = "443")
@IgnoreNoPactsToVerify
public class UserServicePactVerificationTest {

    @BeforeEach
    void setupTarget(PactVerificationContext context) {
        context.setTarget(new HttpTestTarget("localhost", 8080));
    }

    @State("user 42 exists")
    public void userExists() {
        // Seed the DB or mock the repository so GET /users/42 returns Alice
        userRepository.save(new User(42L, "Alice", "alice@example.com"));
    }

    @TestTemplate
    @ExtendWith(PactVerificationInvocationContextProvider.class)
    void pactVerificationTestTemplate(PactVerificationContext context) {
        context.verifyInteraction();
    }
}
```

This test pulls all consumer contracts from the broker, starts UserService on port 8080, replays each request, and verifies responses match. If the API changes (field renamed, field removed, status code changed), the test fails.

**`can-i-deploy` gate in CI:**

```bash
pact-broker can-i-deploy \
  --pacticipant=UserService \
  --version=$GIT_SHA \
  --to-environment=production
```

Returns success only if all consumer contracts for the current UserService version are verified. Prevents deploying a producer that would break consumers in production.

**Producer side (defines the contract in Groovy/YAML/Java)**

```groovy
// src/test/resources/contracts/getUser.groovy
Contract.make {
    description "should return user 42"
    request {
        method 'GET'
        url '/users/42'
        headers { contentType(applicationJson()) }
    }
    response {
        status OK()
        body([
            id: 42,
            name: 'Alice',
            email: 'alice@example.com'
        ])
        headers { contentType(applicationJson()) }
    }
}
```

Plugin configuration in `pom.xml`:

```xml
<plugin>
  <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-cloud-contract-maven-plugin</artifactId>
  <extensions>true</extensions>
  <configuration>
    <baseClassForTests>com.example.UserServiceContractBase</baseClassForTests>
  </configuration>
</plugin>
```

The plugin auto-generates JUnit tests that replay each contract's request against the real controller. It also packages the contracts as a `stubs` JAR in Maven.

**Consumer side (uses producer's stubs)**

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-contract-stub-runner</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

```java
@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureStubRunner(
    ids = "com.example:user-service:+:stubs:8080",
    stubsMode = StubRunnerProperties.StubsMode.REMOTE,
    repositoryRoot = "https://artifactory.internal")
public class OrderServiceTest {

    @Autowired UserClient userClient;

    @Test
    void shouldFetchUser() {
        User u = userClient.getUser(42L);
        assertThat(u.getName()).isEqualTo("Alice");
    }
}
```

Stub Runner starts WireMock on port 8080 with stubs matching the producer's contracts. Consumer tests run against the stub; if the producer's contract changes, consumer's next build pulls new stubs and either passes (compatible) or fails (incompatible).

**Key difference from Pact**: Spring Cloud Contract is producer-driven by default (producer owns the contract; consumer consumes stubs). Pact is consumer-driven (consumer expresses expectations; producer verifies). Spring Cloud Contract can also do consumer-driven with explicit contract publishing.

**Producer pom.xml:**

```xml
<dependencies>
  <dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-contract-verifier</artifactId>
    <scope>test</scope>
  </dependency>
</dependencies>

<build>
  <plugins>
    <plugin>
      <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-cloud-contract-maven-plugin</artifactId>
      <version>4.1.4</version>
      <extensions>true</extensions>
      <configuration>
        <baseClassForTests>com.example.order.BaseContractTest</baseClassForTests>
        <testFramework>JUNIT5</testFramework>
        <contractsDirectory>src/test/resources/contracts</contractsDirectory>
      </configuration>
    </plugin>
  </plugins>
</build>
```

**Contract in Groovy DSL** — `src/test/resources/contracts/orders/shouldReturnOrder.groovy`:

```groovy
package contracts.orders

import org.springframework.cloud.contract.spec.Contract

Contract.make {
    description("should return order by id")
    request {
        method GET()
        url "/orders/42"
        headers { contentType(applicationJson()) }
    }
    response {
        status OK()
        headers { contentType(applicationJson()) }
        body([
            id: 42,
            customerId: "cust-7",
            status: "CONFIRMED",
            totalCents: 19900
        ])
        bodyMatchers {
            jsonPath('$.id', byRegex('[0-9]+').asInteger())
            jsonPath('$.status', byRegex('CONFIRMED|PENDING|CANCELLED'))
        }
    }
}
```

YAML is supported as an alternative; Groovy is more expressive (regex matchers, dynamic values).

**Base test class** — the generated tests extend this; you set up the Spring context and inject the controller into MockMvc/WebTestClient:

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = MOCK)
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
public abstract class BaseContractTest {

    @Autowired MockMvc mockMvc;
    @MockBean OrderService orderService;

    @BeforeEach
    void setup() {
        when(orderService.getOrder(42L)).thenReturn(
            new Order(42L, "cust-7", "CONFIRMED", 19_900));
        RestAssuredMockMvc.mockMvc(mockMvc);
    }
}
```

**What `mvn verify` produces:**
- `target/generated-test-sources/contracts/.../OrdersTest.java` — JUnit test named after the contract filename, extending `BaseContractTest`
- `target/<artifactId>-<version>-stubs.jar` — WireMock mappings for every contract

Run the producer CI: if `OrderController` drifts (returns `status="NEW"` instead of `"CONFIRMED"`), the generated test fails. If contracts are malformed, plugin fails. The stub JAR is published to the Maven repo alongside the normal artifact.

**Publishing stubs:**

```bash
mvn deploy -DaltDeploymentRepository=stubs::default::https://artifactory.example.com/stubs-local
```

Producer teams typically publish to an artifact manager (Artifactory, Nexus). Git-based publishing is also supported (commit stubs to a Git repo) for teams avoiding artifact infrastructure.

**Consumer pom.xml:**

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-contract-stub-runner</artifactId>
  <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
```

**Consumer test** using `@AutoConfigureStubRunner`:

```java
@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureStubRunner(
    ids = "com.example:order-service:+:stubs:8090",
    stubsMode = StubsMode.REMOTE,
    repositoryRoot = "https://artifactory.example.com/stubs-local"
)
class InventoryServiceIntegrationTest {

    @Autowired InventoryService inventoryService;

    @Test
    void reservesStockForConfirmedOrder() {
        // orderClient internally calls http://localhost:8090/orders/42
        // StubRunner serves the WireMock stub on port 8090
        var result = inventoryService.reserveForOrder(42L);
        assertThat(result.reserved()).isTrue();
    }
}
```

**The `ids` format** is `groupId:artifactId:version:classifier:port`. `+` as version means 'latest', classifier `stubs` matches the producer's stub JAR, and port pins the WireMock server.

**stubsMode options:**
- `REMOTE` — pulls from the configured artifactory (production)
- `LOCAL` — uses `~/.m2` local stubs (developer iteration)
- `CLASSPATH` — stubs on test classpath (tight integration)

**Messaging contracts (Kafka / RabbitMQ):**

SCC supports message contracts identically:

```groovy
Contract.make {
    label "orderCreated"
    input {
        triggeredBy("createOrder()")
    }
    outputMessage {
        sentTo "orders-topic"
        body([orderId: 42, status: "CREATED"])
        headers { header("type", "OrderCreated") }
    }
}
```

The consumer test uses `StubTrigger` to fire the producer-side event and assert consumption. Works with Spring Cloud Stream, Spring Kafka, Spring AMQP.

**Breaking-change workflow in CI:**

1. Producer team changes contract (adds field, renames status enum)
2. Producer CI runs `mvn verify` — generated test may fail if implementation doesn't yet match
3. Producer publishes new stub version
4. Consumer teams' next CI run pulls latest stub (if `version=+` or pinned to LATEST)
5. Consumer tests fail if the consumer's code broke
6. Producer coordinates rollout: new stub version with backward-compat → consumers upgrade → deprecated fields removed

**Version pinning strategy:** some teams pin stub versions explicitly in consumer `ids` to avoid surprise breaks. Others run a daily `+` pull to surface compatibility regressions early. A mature org does both: pinned in release branches, `+` on a compatibility-drift canary job.

**Pact consumer test (Java):**

```java
@ExtendWith(PactConsumerTestExt.class)
@PactTestFor(providerName = "order-service")
class InventoryClientPactTest {

    @Pact(consumer = "inventory-service")
    V4Pact orderExists(PactDslWithProvider builder) {
        return builder
            .given("an order with id 42 exists")
            .uponReceiving("a request for order 42")
                .path("/orders/42")
                .method("GET")
            .willRespondWith()
                .status(200)
                .headers(Map.of("Content-Type", "application/json"))
                .body(newJsonBody(o -> {
                    o.numberType("id", 42);
                    o.stringType("customerId", "cust-7");
                    o.stringMatcher("status", "CONFIRMED|PENDING|CANCELLED", "CONFIRMED");
                }).build())
            .toPact(V4Pact.class);
    }

    @Test
    @PactTestFor(pactMethod = "orderExists")
    void fetchesOrder(MockServer mockServer) {
        var client = new OrderClient(mockServer.getUrl());
        assertThat(client.getOrder(42L).status()).isEqualTo("CONFIRMED");
    }
}
```

Running the test emits `inventory-service-order-service.json`; Pact Maven plugin publishes to the broker.

**Pact provider verification (on producer side):**

```java
@Provider("order-service")
@PactBroker(url = "https://pactflow.example.com",
            authentication = @PactBrokerAuth(token = "${PACT_BROKER_TOKEN}"))
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT)
class OrderProviderVerifyTest {

    @LocalServerPort int port;

    @BeforeEach
    void setTarget(PactVerificationContext ctx) {
        ctx.setTarget(new HttpTestTarget("localhost", port));
    }

    @TestTemplate
    @ExtendWith(PactVerificationInvocationContextProvider.class)
    void pactVerification(PactVerificationContext ctx) { ctx.verifyInteraction(); }

    @State("an order with id 42 exists")
    void orderExists() {
        orderRepo.save(new Order(42L, "cust-7", "CONFIRMED", 19_900));
    }
}
```

Provider CI runs this; it pulls all contracts, replays requests, reports pass/fail to the broker.

**SCC equivalent — producer writes the contract:**

```groovy
// producer-repo: src/test/resources/contracts/orders/getOrder.groovy
Contract.make {
    request {
        method GET(); url "/orders/42"
    }
    response {
        status OK()
        body([id: 42, customerId: "cust-7", status: "CONFIRMED"])
        bodyMatchers {
            jsonPath('$.status', byRegex('CONFIRMED|PENDING|CANCELLED'))
        }
    }
}
```

```java
// consumer-repo test
@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureStubRunner(
    ids = "com.example:order-service:+:stubs:8090",
    stubsMode = StubsMode.REMOTE,
    repositoryRoot = "https://artifactory.example.com/stubs-local")
class InventoryServiceStubTest {
    @Test void fetchesOrder() {
        assertThat(orderClient.getOrder(42L).status()).isEqualTo("CONFIRMED");
    }
}
```

**Pact Broker's `can-i-deploy`:**

```bash
pact-broker can-i-deploy \
  --pacticipant inventory-service --version 1.3.0 \
  --to-environment production
```

Outputs a compatibility matrix: can v1.3 of inventory-service safely deploy to prod given all providers' prod versions? If any pact is unverified against current prod provider, the command fails and blocks the deploy.

SCC has no native equivalent. You gain this either by manual pinning (consumer declares exact stub version) or by implementing external release gating.
